  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 220 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMonday sees the majority of the upper level support and moisture \nlift north of the area. Dry, sinking air on the backside of the \nsystem moves in which dampens rain potently. The best signal for \nrain remains to the west in Carbon County. Winds stay gusty behind \nthe system. Global model ensembles have between 50 and 60 percent of \nmembers with wind gusts above climate average for this time of year. \nOur in-house high wind ML model is giving elevated wind gusts around \n40-50 mph for the Arlington and Rawlins wind prone areas. Some spots \nalong I-25 could get some of these gusts. Nothing quite reaching \nhigh wind criteria at this time, though trends are slightly \nincreasing.\n\nOvernight Monday the trough over the West Coast moves eastward. \nAhead of the system the subtropical jet intensifies with the \ntightening of 250 mbar pressure gradients between the trough and an \nupper level ridge over TX and northern Mexico. Being on the left \nexit region of the jet could increase synoptic lift. PWATs are in \nthe 90 to 99 percentile, which is about half an inch. Forecast \nsoundings have most of this moisture residing below 500 mbar, so \nshallow showers are anticipated with low rainfall rates mainly in \nour western areas.\n\nWe see a weak return flow at the surface Tuesday in response to the \nPacific system moving east and surface lee cyclogenesis starting. A \nlull in rain is possible given being removed from the favorable \nsynoptic lifting provided by the jet and subtle ridging nosing in. \nMost shower activity, if we see it during the day, would still be \nkept to Carbon County. By the evening and overnight the advancing \nsystem should be close enough to start kicking off showers. The \nreturn flow could moisten the surface layer enough that we see some \nareas of fog develop in the central and eastern parts of our area \ntowards morning.\n\nBy Wednesday and Thursday we hit a forecast horizon with the \nprogression of this trough. The general trend has been towards a \nslower system, though models like the GFS are a little more \nprogressive and amplified, showing a more negative tilt to the \ntrough shape. Perhaps this increases lift and instability to produce \nthunderstorms over the Nebraska Panhandle Wednesday. But if you go \nwith the Euro solution the trough is not as amplified. The Euro and \nCanadian model solutions are for the system to then retrograde back \nwest and not move much through the remainder of the forecast period. \nAdditionally, some major disagreement in the 00Z runs of global \nmodels on a clipper like system swinging southeast out of Canada. \nGFS, and even the Euro AIFS, do resolve the clipper and ejects the \ntrough northeast. This has major implications to the long range \nforecast. If the clipper solution is correct colder air can be \nanticipated with the potential of some locations seeing freezing \novernight temperatures. But if this is not correct, continued mild \ntemperatures are likely. The features that cause this uncertainty \nappears to be the strength of the ridge to the north in Canada and if \nit allows a trough ejection to occur near Alaska around October \n14th. So, watching the progression of these features around that \ntimeframe in those locations should help resolve what our long term \nforecast.",
